{"causes":["The development of ancient Greek poetry was heavily influenced by the oral traditions of the ancient Greeks.","The invention of the alphabet in the 8th century BCE provided the means for the widespread dissemination of hexameter throughout the Mediterranean region.","The influence of the Persian Wars on Western literature led to a renewed interest in epic poetry and the use of hexameter as a poetic form."],"effects":["The widespread adoption of the hexameter by Latin poets like Virgil and Ovid contributed significantly to the development of Roman poetry.","The continued use of the hexameter in classical studies has ensured its relevance in contemporary scholarship.","The influence of hexameter on modern poetry can be seen in the works of many notable poets, including T.S. Eliot and Ezra Pound."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Homer","sourceName":"Homer","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"hexameter","targetName":"hexameter","context":"The use of hexameter in the Iliad and Odyssey showcased its versatility as a poetic form."},{"sourceSlug":"Virgil","sourceName":"Virgil","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"hexameter","targetName":"hexameter","context":"The widespread adoption of the hexameter by Virgil in his Aeneid contributed significantly to its popularity."},{"sourceSlug":"Ovid","sourceName":"Ovid","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"hexameter","targetName":"hexameter","context":"The extensive use of hexameter in Ovid's Metamorphoses exemplified its continued relevance in classical studies."},{"sourceSlug":"T.S. Eliot","sourceName":"T.S. Eliot","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"hexameter","targetName":"hexameter","context":"The use of hexameter in T.S. Eliot's The Waste Land demonstrated its continued influence on modern poetry."},{"sourceSlug":"Greek mythology","sourceName":"Greek mythology","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"hexameter","targetName":"hexameter","context":"The use of hexameter in the Iliad and Odyssey was heavily influenced by Greek mythology."},{"sourceSlug":"Latin poetry","sourceName":"Latin poetry","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"hexameter","targetName":"hexameter","context":"The development of Latin poetry built upon the foundations laid by ancient Greek poets who used hexameter."},{"sourceSlug":"Ancient Greece","sourceName":"Ancient Greece","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"hexameter","targetName":"hexameter","context":"The use of hexameter in ancient Greek poetry marked its origins as a poetic form."},{"sourceSlug":"Epic poetry","sourceName":"Epic poetry","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"hexameter","targetName":"hexameter","context":"The use of hexameter in epic poetry is closely tied to the development of classical literature."},{"sourceSlug":"Rome","sourceName":"Rome","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"hexameter","targetName":"hexameter","context":"The use of hexameter in Roman poetry was heavily influenced by ancient Greek and Etruscan traditions."}],"places":[{"name":"Ancient Greece","role":"Birthplace"},{"name":"Rome","role":"Major influence"},{"name":"Persia","role":"Influence"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: hexameter","type":"Reference article","slug":"hexameter"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q109548","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hexameter"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"hexameter contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"Hexameter (from Greek ἑξάμετρον, 'six measures') is the oldest and most prestigious metrical form in Greek and Latin poetry — a dactylic verse metre consisting of six feet, where each foot is either a dactyl (one long syllable followed by two short syllables, — ∪ ∪) or a spondee (two long syllables, — —), with the fifth foot almost always a dactyl and the sixth foot always a spondee or trochee. Hexameter is the metre of Homer's Iliad and Odyssey (8th century BCE), Hesiod's Theogony and Works and Days, Apollonius of Rhodes's Argonautica, and Virgil's Aeneid and Georgics — the metre in which the foundational epic narratives of Greek and Roman civilisation were composed and transmitted. The specific pattern ∪ ∪ / — ∪ ∪ / — ∪ ∪ / — ∪ ∪ / — ∪ ∪ / — × was known in antiquity as the 'heroic metre' (versus heroicus) because it was identified as the metre of heroic epic narrative.

The hexameter's origins are disputed — it may derive from an early Greek oral poetic tradition that predates the Linear B script, possibly from a merger of shorter metrical units, or it may have been imported from an early Near Eastern metrical tradition. Its quantitative character — founded on the distinction between long and short syllables rather than the stress-accent patterns of Germanic and modern European prosody — made it fundamentally different from the accentual-syllabic metres that dominate modern European poetry. When Latin poets adapted hexameter (Ennius, c. 239–169 BCE, was the first to use it systematically in Latin), they adapted the quantitative Greek system to the Latin language, creating the tradition of Latin quantitative verse that dominated Roman literary culture and influenced medieval Latin poetry.

Hexameter was used not only for epic but for didactic poetry (Hesiod, Lucretius, Virgil's Georgics), hymns (the Homeric Hymns), oracle verse (the Delphic Oracle's responses were traditionally in hexameter), and philosophical poetry (Empedocles, Lucretius). The hexameter's long history — from Homer to Virgil to the medieval Latin tradition, and to ambitious modern adaptations (Longfellow's Evangeline, 1847, in English hexameter) — makes it the most historically consequential metrical form in Western literary tradition.","subjects":["Classical Literature","Ancient Era","Greek Poetry","Latin Poetry","Prosody","Homer","Epic Poetry","Literary Form"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The hexameter's influence on classical literature and its continued relevance in contemporary scholarship make it a significant entity in the arc of world history.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
